      "question": {
        ":question/id": "c7dca190-8161-5638-80ae-d88ed136779c",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\left(2\\right)y=4,\\quad y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\nAn integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=2$ and $Q(x)=4.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =e^{\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =e^{2 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)=e^{2 x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e^{2 x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y) & =e^{2 x}\\cdot 4 \\\\ e^{2 x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2 e^{2 x}y & =4 e^{2 x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ye^{2 x}) & =4 e^{2 x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ye^{2 x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4 e^{2 x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ ye^{2 x} & =4\\left(\\frac{1}{2} e^{2 x}\\right)+C \\\\ ye^{2 x} & =2 e^{2 x}+C \\\\ y & =\\left(C + 2 e^{2 x}\\right) e^{- 2 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =\\frac{(2 e^{2 (0)})+C}{(e^{2 (0)})} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{2+C}{1} \\\\ C & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2 - 3 e^{- 2 x}.\n$$",
